p75 validation protocol (Lane R, Phase 1, T3 -- paper only)      2026-11-06
Nothing in this file was executed live.  It defines how the p74 canary winners and
the OFF_ENTITY_* candidates get PROVEN once the lead approves live reads (0x124
history: lead-approved windows only, one device handle per run, no game start by
this lane).  Canary values below are the p74 v3 canary (ext\p74_matrix.py,
_ritual\p74_matrix.out): measured noise 0.0/MB on static .data/.rdata and
0.29/MB on live entity-cluster pages (v1 loose 448/MB -> v2 409/MB -> v3 with
orthogonality 0.29/MB; v1/v2 evidence: p74_matrix_v1_loose.out,
p74_matrix_v2_noortho.out).

P74 V3 CANARY (the reference definition for everything below)
  hard gates, all required:
    1. all 16 floats finite
    2. |m[15]| > 1e-6
    3. |m[0]| and |m[5]| each in [0.3, 3.0]          (FOV-plausible)
    4. |m[11]| > 1e-6 or |m[14]| > 1e-6               (perspective shape)
    5. magnitude sanity: forward-row norm in the row band; |m[11]| < 100;
       |m[12]|,|m[13]|,|m[14]| each < 1e6            (kills FLT_MAX tables)
    6. row bands: tier A [0.7, 1.4] (pure view, near-unit rows) or
       tier B [0.3, 3.0] (view-projection, FOV-scaled rows) -- right, up AND
       forward rows all in the same band
    7. orthogonality: |dot(r,u)|, |dot(r,f)|, |dot(u,f)| each < 0.05
  ranking: soft score (row norms near 1, m[3]==m[7]==0, |m[15]-1|<1e-3 pure-view
  marker, |m[15]|>1 VP marker, orthogonality margin, translation present).
  ANTI-EXAMPLE stays 0x022EE01 = VTP_GetEngineTraceClient CODE (p74 printed the
  16 floats there: they fail gates 3 -- |m[0]|=1.3e-18, |m[5]|=3.6e+16).

================================================================================
(a) VIEW MATRIX -- rotate-90 differential
================================================================================
GOAL: find the single live 16-float block that is the engine's view (or view-
projection) matrix, killing code-RVAs, static tables and bone rest poses in one
shot.  The p74 addendum proved why: byte-stable rotation blocks exist in entity
pages (skeleton bind poses); only the real matrix CHANGES with the camera.

PREREQ (lead-approved live window, number p76+ -- ask the lead, p73-p75 are
taken; the crash-hunt's p85-p110 were a different namespace):
  1. One live .data dump via ext\p72_ppath.py --stage=map --pid=<pid>
     --target=<pa> (writes temp\p72_data.bin keyed pid+target, R5: copy it to
     temp\p7x_data_<runid>_A.bin before anything else) -- OR dumplive.py for a
     whole-image dump if the section walk is healthier that day.  Single
     elevated process, one \\.\GIOV3 handle, no concurrent python (0x124 rule).
  2. ext\p74_matrix.py --region=temp\p7x_data_<runid>_A.bin:0x14334C000
     --out=_ritual\p7x_rot_<runid>_A.out   (region base = live module .data VA;
     the current build's .data VA/RVA from the PE table, adjust per build).

PROCEDURE (one variable per run, R5 -- each run its own output file):
  Scan A:  player stands still, camera arbitrary, dump + scan (file ..._A.out).
  Control: 10 s later, NO rotation, dump + scan (..._A2.out).  Acceptance for
           the control: candidate VAs identical to A and dot(r_A, r_A2) within
           0.1 of +-1 (basis unchanged -- confirms pipeline stability and that
           the matrix does not move VA between frames).
  Rotate:  turn the camera exactly 90 deg of yaw (keyboard turn, no strafe, no
           walk -- translation must stay put), settle >= 5 s.
  Scan B:  dump + scan (..._B.out).
  Differential acceptance -- a candidate survives iff ALL of:
    (i)   it passes the p74 v3 canary in A, A2 and B;
    (ii)  same VA in all three (the matrix churns values, never address);
    (iii) right and forward rows are 90 deg apart between A and B:
          |dot(r_A, r_B)| < 0.1 and |dot(f_A, f_B)| < 0.1;
    (iv)  up row unchanged: dot(u_A, u_B) > 0.95 (yaw keeps the up axis);
    (v)   translation row unchanged within 1.0 world unit (|t_A - t_B| < 1.0
          per component -- you did not move);
    (vi)  it is NOT byte-identical between A and B (static table / bind pose
          dies here -- the p74 addendum case).
  Code-RVAs die at (i) (machine-code bytes are not finite-plausible float blocks
  with orthogonal rows); static float tables and skeleton poses die at (vi);
  heap noise dies at (iii).  Anything surviving all six IS the matrix.
  Repeat once (B2, second 90 deg turn to 180 deg) if two candidates survive --
  the true matrix follows the rotation both times.

CADENCE + RUN-ID CONVENTIONS:
  runid = p7x_<YYYYMMDD_HHMM> (x = allocated number).  Files, never reused:
    temp\p7x_data_<runid>_{A,A2,B}.bin     region dumps (copies of p72_data.bin)
    _ritual\p7x_rot_<runid>_{A,A2,B}.out    p74_matrix.py --region/--out outputs
    _ritual\p7x_diff_<runid>.out            the (i)-(vi) table: one line per
                                             candidate VA, PASS/FAIL per gate
  The diff is a new ext\p7x script (same canary import from p74_matrix.py);
  it re-runs p74_matrix.py's canary on both dumps, never re-reads live memory
  itself (the differential is computed from the two files, so a crash between
  A and B loses one scan, not the session).
  One live window covers one A/A2/B triple; if the game or box dies mid-run,

================================================================================
(b) ENTITY FIELDS -- health / team / origin (candidates 0x3A0 / 0x478 / 0x258)
================================================================================
All three are CANDIDATES until each passes its section here.  Anchors are the
name-needle clusters (p60 name scans); the p61/p63/p64 scripts already exist.

HEALTH (candidate OFF_ENTITY_HEALTH = 0x3A0) -- damage-window differential,
the p61_live.py technique exactly:
  python ext\p61_live.py --needle=<player> --pre=6 --post=8
         --last=temp\p60_name_u16.txt --out=temp\p7x_health_<runid>.txt
  Sequence: anchor rescan around the remembered cluster -> pre-snapshot of the
  hit pages -> countdown beeps -> grenade/self-damage -> 8 post-snapshots at
  1.5 s -> diff.  A health dword must DROP by >= 3 (>= 1 with --loose) across
  the damage step and stay flat afterwards (p61's filter: seq[i-1]-seq[i] >=
  need, then flat inside the hold window; shield absorbs first, so health
  falls only after the shield hits 0 -- the p64_pairs --chain shape).
  ACCEPTANCE for offset 0x3A0: the dropping dword sits at entity+0x3A0 for the
  SAME anchored entity in TWO independent damage windows (two runids).  A drop
  at a different delta means the candidate offset is wrong -- record the true
  delta, that becomes the new candidate, one more window confirms it.

TEAM (candidate OFF_ENTITY_TEAM = 0x478) -- duo-partner check:
  p64_pairs.py --seed mode pairs entities (health/shield clusters).  Read
  +0x478 of the LOCAL player and of the DUO PARTNER (anchor by partner name
  needle): both must hold the same small integer (0..64) in the same match,
  while an ENEMY's +0x478 (enemy name anchor) holds a different value.
  ACCEPTANCE for 0x478: equal-for-duo / different-for-enemy in TWO matches
  (team ids reshuffle between matches; the offset must survive the reshuffle).
  Evidence files: temp\p7x_team_<runid>.out with the three reads + verdict.

ORIGIN (candidate OFF_ENTITY_ORIGIN = 0x258) -- movement plausibility, the
p63_pos.py technique:
  python ext\p63_pos.py --needle=<player> --walk=6 --out=temp\p7x_pos_<runid>.txt
  The 3 floats at +0x258 must (i) be finite, (ii) change smoothly while the
  player walks (|delta per 1.5 s sample| <= ~10 world units, run speed ~5.5),
  (iii) stay flat while standing still, (iv) track DIRECTION (walk north ->
  y grows monotonically for the walk window).  A coordinate triple that jumps
  wildly or never moves is a transform/table, not the origin.
  INTEGRATION CHECK (the real verdict): once (a) has a matrix winner, run the
  features.cpp world-to-screen on entity+0x258 of a VISIBLE enemy through the
  validated matrix: the projected point must land INSIDE the viewport while the
  enemy is on screen, and slide off the correct side when they leave.  One
  candidate origin that projects correctly with the winning matrix closes both
  (a) and (b) for the ESP chain end-to-end.

General evidence rules for (b): every read comes back through the gio device in
one process per window; every verdict cites its file; two independent windows
per field before anything lands in memory.h.


================================================================================
(c) PHASE 3 LANDING -- OffsetValidator canaries + memory.h procedure
================================================================================
No memory.h / offset_validator.cpp edits happen this phase (Phase 1 is paper).
When the (a)/(b) winners exist, they land like this:

C1. WINNER TABLE (each row: constant, winner value, evidence file, verdict):
      ApexOffsets::VIEW_MATRIX    <matrix RVA>   _ritual\p7x_rot_<runid>_{A,B}.out
      ApexOffsets::ENTITY_LIST     <slot  RVA>   (see C3)
      ApexOffsets::LOCAL_PLAYER   <slot  RVA>   (see C3)
      OFF_ENTITY_ORIGIN/TEAM/HEALTH <0x258-ish>  temp\p7x_{pos,team,health}_<runid>.out
    The lead sign-off is recorded in STATE.md (lead single-writes state).

C2. VIEW MATRIX canary as OffsetValidator will encode it (exact values -- the
    p74 v3 canary plus the rotate-90 proof):
      ValidateViewMatrixRead() (src\offset_validator.cpp, replace the current
      3-check body):
        - ReadBytes 64 at moduleBase + VIEW_MATRIX, fail -> "unreadable"
        - all 16 finite                       -> "matrix contains non-finite values"
        - |m[15]| > 1e-6                     -> "w-row is zero: looks like code
                                                (VTP_GetEngineTraceClient)"
        - |m[0]|,|m[5]| in [0.3, 3.0]        -> "FOV scale out of band"
        - |m[11]| > 1e-6 or |m[14]| > 1e-6    -> "no perspective shape"
        - row bands: right/up/forward each in [0.7,1.4] (tier A) or [0.3,3.0]
          (tier B)                           -> "row norm out of band"
        - |m[11]| < 100 and |m[12..14]| < 1e6 -> "unbounded magnitudes (table)"
        - |dot(r,u)|,|dot(r,f)|,|dot(u,f)| < 0.05 -> "rows not orthogonal (table)"
      Every failure names its gate (the current code style already does this for
      the w-row; extend the rest the same way).  features.cpp UpdateESP keeps
      the same canary inline (it already early-returns on failure -- it just
      gets the full gate list).
      LIVE EVIDENCE per offset: the validator's verdict line in the run log
      PLUS the p7x differential file; a canary that passes only statically is
      not a landing -- the rotate-90 file is part of the definition of DONE.

C3. POINTER canaries (existing checks, tightened):
      ENTITY_LIST: ReadPointer at moduleBase+ENTITY_LIST must be a user-space
        pointer AND the head entry must itself walk (already implemented), AND
        while in a match at least one non-null entry beyond the head (count>0)
        -> verdict "entity list empty (not in a match)" vs "not a pointer".
      LOCAL_PLAYER: slot pointer readable, target readable (implemented), PLUS
        entity-shape: origin triple finite at +OFF_ENTITY_ORIGIN and health in
        [1,200] at +OFF_ENTITY_HEALTH -> "does not look like a player object".

C4. FIELD canaries (per-offset verdicts with live evidence):
      HEALTH: int in [1,200]; drops across a logged damage window (validator
        logs two reads 2 s apart when a damage event is flagged -- evidence file
        temp\p7x_health_<runid>.out cited in the verdict string).
      TEAM: int in [0,64); equals LOCAL_PLAYER's team for the duo partner
        (verdict string cites temp\p7x_team_<runid>.out).
      ORIGIN: 3 finite floats, |coord| < 1e5, world-to-screen depth > 0.1
        (cites temp\p7x_pos_<runid>.out + the integration check).

C5. memory.h LANDING PROCEDURE (Phase 3, lane R's exclusive files):
      1. Lead approves the winner table (STATE.md entry).
      2. One constant per edit (R5: one variable per probe): VIEW_MATRIX first
         (ESP is dead without it), then ENTITY_LIST, LOCAL_PLAYER, then the
         OFF_ENTITY_* trio.  Each edit: comment above the constant cites the
         evidence file path; 0x022EE01 REMAINS in R5AC_VMT::
         GET_ENGINE_TRACE_CLIENT (it is the correct code RVA -- only the
         VIEW_MATRIX alias changes meaning) and the old value is kept in the
         comment as the documented history.
      3. After each edit: build (QwenKernPremium gate), inject, run
         OffsetValidator with the game up, read the per-offset verdict lines
         from the run output file (R3: the file is the verdict), and only then
         let UpdateESP publish boxes for that offset's subtree.
      4. If a later game patch breaks a canary, the verdict line names the gate
         that failed -- re-run the (a)/(b) section for that offset only.

ACCEPTANCE FOR THE WHOLE PROTOCOL: ESP boxes on real entities, every offset in
memory.h carrying a comment with its live-evidence file, and every validator
verdict PASS with the game in a match -- read back from output files, not chat.

RESULT: protocol complete -- (a) rotate-90 differential with 6 acceptance gates + control scan, (b) health/team/origin procedures on p61/p63/p64 with two-window acceptance, (c) exact p74-v3 canary values for OffsetValidator + one-constant-per-edit memory.h landing
VERDICT: p75 is paper-only (no live reads this phase); the matrix hunt runs the (a) differential in a lead-approved live window (numbers p76+ to be requested), entity fields run p61_live/p63_pos/p64_pairs as-is, and Phase 3 lands winners one constant per edit with per-gate verdict strings and evidence-file comments in include\memory.h

  stop (0x124 discipline), file what exists, ask the lead before re-arming.
